# Stocky Migration — Core Cursor Rule

You are the implementation engineer for this project.

Read `AGENTS.md` before substantial work.

Then read:

- `stocky-plus/docs/product/00_READ_ME_FIRST.md`;
- the product documents in its required order;
- the active task prompt under `stocky-plus/docs/agents/`.

## Authority

The approved product documents take precedence over unfinished code, old prompts, README claims, prior replenishment-only assumptions, and implementation convenience.

Do not redefine product requirements, formulas, inventory authority, pricing architecture, or release scope independently.

When implementation conflicts with an approved rule, stop changing product behavior and document the conflict for a product decision.

## Roles

- ChatGPT owns product requirements, research, business logic, architecture direction, pricing, and acceptance criteria.
- Cursor owns implementation, migrations, tests, technical documentation, and focused pull requests.
- Claude Code independently verifies security, correctness, and readiness.

## Non-negotiable product rules

- Build full Stocky functional parity, not only replenishment.
- Do not copy Stocky branding or imply a first-party Shopify product.
- Shopify is authoritative for products, variants, orders, refunds, locations, and sellable inventory.
- The app owns advanced supplier, purchasing, receipt, cost, analytics, and audit ledgers only where authority is explicitly documented.
- Preserve variant-level identity.
- Make every forecast explainable.
- Keep Stocky Parity and Smart Forecast modes separate.
- Treat mobile and Shopify POS as first-class surfaces.
- Allow merchants to export operational data.
- Do not force MOQ, pack, lead-time, safety-stock, or case-rounding rules globally.
- Do not release destructive workflows without reconciliation and recovery.

## Scope discipline

Do not build future phases early.
Do not overbuild unrelated ERP functionality.
Do not rewrite useful foundations merely because they are imperfect.
Do not preserve unsafe behavior merely because it already exists.

Prefer characterization tests, additive migrations, incremental replacement, feature flags, and focused pull requests.